 ### Priority
 
@@ -130,6 +130,19 @@ ### Call Panel (attendant)
 
 After each call, the ticket leaves the waiting queue and enters the session/display call history.
 
+### Call-next implementation
+
+`App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (invokable) picks and marks the winner for the call panel. It receives the queue, the attendant `User` and an `App\Enums\CallMode` (`any`, `priority_only`, `normal_only`; default `any`) and returns the updated `Ticket`.
+
+- Candidates are only `waiting` tickets of the requested queue; other queues and already `called` tickets are never selected.
+- Ordering is deterministic: `priority` descending, then `issued_at` ascending, then `id` ascending.
+- `any` applies the full ordering plus the 3-call fairness rule; `priority_only` only considers `priority > 0`; `normal_only` only considers `priority = 0` and calls the oldest first. Fairness is ignored outside `any`, but calls made in any mode count in its history.
+- Fairness: when the last three calls of the queue (most recent first by `called_at`, then `id` desc) are all priority and a normal ticket is waiting, the call is restricted to normal tickets. A normal call resets the streak; with no normal waiting, priority tickets keep being called.
+- An inactive queue is rejected with `App\Exceptions\InactiveQueueException`; no eligible candidate raises `App\Exceptions\NoWaitingTicketsException`.
+- The action runs in a transaction. It locks the queue row, reads the ordered candidates, then locks each candidate and re-checks it is still `waiting` before marking it, so a ticket called concurrently through `Ticket::call()` is skipped and the next candidate is attempted. The winner is updated only in `status`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id`, and the returned model already reflects the call.
+
+Authorization, HTTP endpoints, the Livewire panel, display/TV broadcasting, recall and skip are out of scope; the caller is trusted.
+
